      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the area of the region \\( S \\) which is the set of points in the complex plane that are inverses of some point in the region \\( R \\), where \\( R \\) is the region outside the regular hexagon centered at the origin with opposite sides one unit apart and one pair of sides parallel to the imaginary axis.\n\n### Step 1: Understanding the Geometry of the Hexagon\nA regular hexagon centered at the origin with opposite sides one unit apart and one pair of sides parallel to the imaginary axis can be described as follows:\n\n- The vertices of the hexagon are at \\( z = \\pm i, \\pm 1, \\pm 1/2 \\).\n\n### Step 2: Equation of the Hexagon \nWe can describe the hexagon \\( R \\) using equations. \nFor the vertices \\( i, -i, 1, 1/2, -1/2 \\), we parametrize the boundary of the hexagon as:\n\\[ |z| = 1 \\]\nwhere \\( z \\) can be expressed as a mix of imaginary and real numbers giving us:\n\\[ z = x + iy \\]\nwith \\( x^2 + y^2 = 1 \\) for one pair, inside, and:\n\\[ z = x - iy \\]\nfor one pair containing the real axis only.\n\n### Step 3: Reflecting over the Real Axis\nNow, we need to find points outside this hexagon, i.e., those of the form:\n\\[ \\frac{1}{z} = w \\]\nsuch that \\( w \\) is outside the bounded study.\n\nWe address how \\( w \\) lands outside of the polynomial:\n\\[\nw = \\frac{1}{z} \\implies |w| = \\frac{1}{|z|}\n\\]\nFor \\( w \\) to be outside of the hexagon, \\( \\frac{1}{|z|} > 1 \\), thus implying \\( |z| < 1 \\). Therefore, we need to determine where \\( |z| = 1 \\) and \\( |z| > 1 \\).\n\n### Step 4: Squares and Regions\nWe realize that transforming \\( z \\) to \\( 1/z \\) in terms of squares can often be beneficial. If \\( w = \\frac{1}{z} \\):\n\\[ |w| = \\frac{1}{|z|} \\]\nThus, since \\( z \\) is inside the unit disk: \\( |z| < 1 \\)  gets mapped to \\( |w| > 1 \\)  by the given transform. If \\( |z| > 1 \\), then \\( |w| < 1 \\).\n\nTherefore, all \\( w \\) such that \\( |w| > 1 \\) should cover the region of \\( S \\). \n\nCalculating the area:\n\\[ \\frac{1}{z} \\] will make \\( S \\) into another hexagon but marginalized:\n\\[ |z| < 1 \\rightarrow 0 < z = \\frac{1}{w} < 1 \\]\n\nHence, the region \\( R \\) has an area 6 units squared; therefore:\n\\[ S \\] being its inversion will recast to clearly feature float as \\( \\boxed{1} \\) units squared.",
