    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "In the complex plane, the hexagon represents $|z-x|=1$. The set $S$ is obtained by taking the inverse of every point $z$ in the dminagonal. Since evey polynomial $P$ of degree n can have at most $n$ real roots, there are at most 6 imaginary roots for $Q(1/z)=P(1/z)$. Hence, $S$ is a triangle of area 1, originating from the origin and its base measured along a diameter, hence having radius lying inside the hexagon. The area of a triangle is half its base multiplied by its height, which are, in this case, 2 times the distance between adjacent vertices, and also 2 times the side diagonals of the hexagon. Hence, the area is $\\boxed{6}$.",
