    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We begin by drawing a diagram for a hexagon with center coordinates, $0$, on the complex plane.\n\n[asy]\nfor (int k=-1; k<=1; ++k) {\n   for (int l=-1; l<=1; ++l) {\n    draw((k,l)--(-k,l)--(k,1-l)--(-k,1-l)--(-k,-l)--(k,-l)--(k,1-l)--(-k,1-l)--(-1,k)--(-1,-k)--(0,0)--(0,1));\n  }\n}\n\nfor (int i=0; i<6; ++i) {\n  string fn=\"label at \" + string(i) + \"\\'a=\";\n  label(midpoint(dir(30*i)+(-.2,0)),fn,fontsize(8));\n}\ndot((0,0.2)); // center\ndot((-0.6,0.5)); // the tip of a side\n\ndot((0.2,0.3)); // the tip of the right hand diagonal of opposite opposers\n\n[/asy]\n\nSince the hexagon has opposite pairs of sides that are 1 unit apart and the vertex angles of the hexagon are 30 degrees, the sides are half the side lengths of an equilateral triangle with unit sides. We can then see that the hexagon has equal sides of 1 unit each and vertex angles of 60 degrees, as the angle between parallel sides is 60 degrees. We can also see that the area of a hexagon can be split into six of these 60 degree triangle, each with a side length of 1 unit.\n   \nThe area of an equilateral triangle with side length $s$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}s^2$, so the area of the large triangle is $\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4}$. The areas of the six inner triangles are six times the area of one such triangle, meaning the area of the large triangle minus the area of these six is $\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4}-\\frac{1(3\\sqrt{3})}{2}=0$ and is the area of $S$.",
